<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="trade-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="trade-title">Market by market</h2>
      <p>Descriptions come from press and guides, and markets are varied. The accounting points are our own general analysis. Check tax treatment for your exact goods.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="market-list">
      <article id="gandhi-nagar">
        <h3>Gandhi Nagar: readymade garments</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">Widely described as a garment wholesale market, with Delhi government plans reported in 2022 to develop it as a garment hub, and household units and contractors involved in stitching.</p>
        <p><strong>Raise with your accountant:</strong> sizes and styles in stock, returns from retailers, job work and stitching payments, seasonal stock left over, and GST on garments, as rates can depend on price.</p>
      </article>
      <article id="khari-baoli">
        <h3>Khari Baoli: spices, nuts and groceries</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">A long-established wholesale spice and dry fruit market, where sources describe generations of family traders.</p>
        <p><strong>Raise with your accountant:</strong> different GST treatment by commodity and packaging, weight loss and wastage in stock, bulk cash handling, and food licensing costs.</p>
      </article>
      <article id="sadar-bazaar">
        <h3>Sadar Bazaar: household goods, toys and gifts</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">A general wholesale market. The Swadeshi Market area is described as a cluster of small shops selling toys, artificial jewellery, gifts and plastic goods.</p>
        <p><strong>Raise with your accountant:</strong> many low-value items and how to track them, festive stock peaks, mixed GST rates across a catalogue, and imports if you buy directly.</p>
      </article>
      <article id="chandni-chowk">
        <h3>Chandni Chowk's trading lanes</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">Better seen as many specialist markets. {% ext "https://www.delhitourism.gov.in/shopping/chandni_chowk.html", "Delhi Tourism" %} describes Bhagirath Palace for electrical goods and medical items, and others describe Dariba Kalan for jewellery.</p>
        <p><strong>Raise with your accountant:</strong> your own lane's trade matters more than the address. For jewellers, high-value stock and cash rules. For electricals, warranty returns and brand schemes.</p>
      </article>
      <article id="kashmere-gate">
        <h3>Kashmere Gate: auto parts</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">Directory listings and market guides show many auto parts wholesalers around Hamilton Road and Guru Nanak Auto Market, though we found no official source.</p>
        <p><strong>Raise with your accountant:</strong> a very large number of part codes, dealer credit and claims, and correct goods codes on invoices.</p>
      </article>
      <article id="kirti-nagar">
        <h3>Kirti Nagar: furniture and timber</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">Guides describe a large furniture cluster. The Department of Industries also lists a Kirti Nagar Industrial Area, so some businesses make as well as sell.</p>
        <p><strong>Raise with your accountant:</strong> if you make things, see {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-manufacturers/", "manufacturer accounting" %}. Also advances from customers, delivery and installation, and made-to-order stock.</p>
      </article>
      <article id="karol-bagh">
        <h3>Karol Bagh: mixed retail and electronics</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">A long-established shopping area. Gaffar Market is described as known for phones and electronics, with bridal wear also a part of the area's trade.</p>
        <p><strong>Raise with your accountant:</strong> most businesses here are closer to {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-retail-shops/", "retail shops" %}. For electronics, serial numbers, warranty and second-hand goods need attention.</p>
      </article>
      <article id="lajpat-nagar">
        <h3>Lajpat Nagar: retail, more than wholesale</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">Its Central Market is described as organised around clothing, footwear, housewares and jewellery, sold largely to shoppers.</p>
        <p><strong>Raise with your accountant:</strong> see {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-retail-shops/", "retail shops" %} for daily sales and tender matching.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="common-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="common-title">What nearly all market traders share</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Party ledgers, with receipts matched to bills</li>
        <li>Long credit periods, and the 180-day credit rule on unpaid supplier bills</li>
        <li>GST credit matched with GSTR-2B</li>
        <li>Stock figures that hold up in a count</li>
        <li>Festival peaks that strain whoever does the books</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">The full workflow is on {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-traders/", "accounting for traders" %}.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Choosing someone</h2>
      <p>Ask whether they have handled your goods, your invoice volumes and your busy season. Ask how they would visit or collect paper records from your shop, because lanes in old markets can be hard to reach. See {% routeLink "/guides/how-to-hire-an-accountant-in-delhi/", "how to hire an accountant" %}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

